 "standard_text": "Solve simultaneous linear equations in two unknowns.",
 "meta_desc": "Free CAPS Grade 10 simultaneous equations worksheets: elimination, substitution and word problems. 100 questions with memos.",
 "key_big": "Eliminate one unknown, solve, then substitute back.",
 "key_sub": "Same signs: subtract.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Elimination: add or subtract the equations to remove one unknown.</li><li>Same signs: subtract. Opposite signs: add.</li><li>Multiply a whole equation first if the coefficients do not match.</li><li>Substitution: make one unknown the subject and substitute it into the other equation.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>2x + y = 7 and x − y = 2</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>add: 3x = 9</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: x = 3, y = 1</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wrong operation</td><td>Same signs: subtract.</td></tr><tr><td>One term only</td><td>Multiply every term.</td></tr><tr><td>Only x</td><td>Substitute back to find y.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does CAPS Grade 10 say about simultaneous equations?",
   "a": "Solve simultaneous linear equations in two unknowns."
  },
  {
   "q": "What does the solution mean on a graph?",
   "a": "The point where the two lines cross."
  },
  {
   "q": "Can there be no solution?",
   "a": "Yes, for parallel lines."
  },
  {
   "q": "Which method is best?",
   "a": "Elimination when coefficients match; substitution when one unknown is easy to isolate."
  }
